    COMPILE-2  BasicElse   failed at reached end of input

⚠ WHAT THIS SETTLES: DISPATCH IS LIVE, NOT FROZEN AT BIND. The frozen-at-bind
prediction was that a reader which had already resolved the tokenizer would go
on reading cleanly. It does not. The same body, on the same node, in the same
process, reads fine before the install and reads empty after it. Reading is
re-consulting something the install changed.

⚠ WHAT IT DOES NOT SETTLE, and do not let the strength of it borrow authority
from Arm A: it shows the failure is TEMPORALLY tied to the install. It does not
show the failing read routes through tokenize's body SPECIFICALLY. Any state
the install touches would produce this. Arm A is still owed and is still the
promoter.
⚠ AND NOTE THE COUNT MOVES BETWEEN THE TWO COMPILES -- 42 then 43. That is the
original confound, deliberately reintroduced, and it is why this does not
replace the two-arm swap above. The swap holds the count FIXED and settles
IDENTITY; this holds the population fixed and settles TIME. Neither substitutes
for the other.

ITEM 3 -- THE HOOK CENSUS. Subordinate to neither arm. RUN 2026-08-20, and it
answers the question the two fix shapes turn on: does tokenize have siblings.

    entered fbGen           59
    INSTALLED               43
    dropped at the datA gate 16
    dropped:  ANYtoken Any Attributes InitiatE Looper Modifier NewGroup
              ShortcuT Start continue followedBy loopOnAttributes
              loopOnMembers nameSet numberSet return

⚠ THE ANSWER IS ONE, AND THE REASON IS THE INTERESTING PART. tokenize is the
only bootstrap-constructed machinery rule that REACHES installation. The others
exist -- nameSet, numberSet, counter, delimiter, Modifier, Limit, MEMBERs, Any,
ruleSkipSet are all built in GroupMain.twk the same way -- but every one of
them CARRIES DATA, because they are character sets, and the datA gate drops
them before fbGen ever installs anything. tokenize carries no data. It is a
method hook, and that is precisely why it is the one that gets a body written
over it.

⚠ SO THE SIBLINGS' IMMUNITY IS INCIDENTAL, NOT DESIGNED. They are safe because
of what they happen to hold, not because anything exempts them. A future
machinery rule that is a hook rather than a character set is a second customer
the moment it exists, and nothing in the tree would stop it.

THAT IS WHAT THE CENSUS BUYS, and it is not the clean count it looks like:

    on TODAY'S count of one    defer-the-hook and off-rule-plus-activation are
                               equivalent in effect, and the cheaper wins
    on the REASON for the one  the count is one by accident of data, so the
                               special case is a fix with an expiry date

⚠ AND THE SEARCH SPACE IS NAMED, because an absence claim is only as good as
its search. This censused ONE surface: rules the bootstrap constructs by name
in GroupMain.twk, cross-read against the walk's own ENTER and INSTALL trace. A
rule DECLARED IN THE GRAMMAR that the reader nonetheless invokes would not
appear in it. Whether that class is empty is NOT measured here.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FIX SHAPES: a live dispatch means sequencing alone does not
rescue a read that comes after the install -- so "compile everyone else first,
install tokenize last" is a real candidate fix and NOT merely a re-ordering of
the same failure. That is the fourth-customer shape with self-hosting
preserved, and it is now the better-supported of the two below.

THE TWO FIX SHAPES RIDE BEHIND THE TRACE. Both are candidate-grade. Neither is
recommended, and they differ in what they concede:

    off-rule storage plus explicit activation
        generate tokenize's body like every other citizen, store it OFF the
        rule, and activate only when nothing further needs the old tokenizer.
        This makes tokenize the FOURTH customer of a ruling that already has
        three -- the napalm, the BlocK re-poison, and mid-walk setParse
        binding. ⚠ IT ASSUMES THE MECHANISM. If the trace comes back negative
        this shape is solving a problem that was never there.

    exempt the hook from installation
        never install a generated body over tokenize at all. ⚠ IT CONCEDES
        THAT tokenize NEVER SELF-HOSTS ITS OWN PARSE, which is a ruling about
        the language and not a patch. Cheaper, and it forecloses something.

WHAT WOULD KILL EITHER: a negative trace kills the first outright and leaves
the second as a blunt workaround for an unknown cause. A trace that shows the
read entering the overwritten body promotes the story and makes the choice
between the two a design call rather than a guess.
